  "client_view_text": " A process is a program in execution, which includes the program code, data, and system resources allocated to it. A thread is a flow of execution within a process, which shares the same memory space and resources as the process. In other words, a process is a container that holds one or more threads. Here are some key differences between processes and threads:\n\n1.  **Process**: A process is a separate entity that runs independently of other processes. It has its own memory space, and each process has its own copy of the program code, data, and system resources. When a process is created, a new memory space is allocated A process and a thread are two fundamental concepts in operating systems that are often confused with each other. A process is a program in execution, while a thread is a subset of the process that can execute instructions independently.\nA process is a program in execution, which includes its own memory space, open files, and system resources. Each process has its own program counter, stack, and local variables. When a process is created, the operating system allocates a new memory space for it, and the process runs independently of other processes.\nOn the other hand, a thread is a subset of a process. It is a flow of execution within a process. A thread shares the same memory space as the process, and it has its own program counter, stack, and local variables. Threads are lightweight processes that can run concurrently with other threads within the same process.\nHere are some key differences between processes and threads:\nProcess Thread\n1. A process is a program in execution, while a thread is a subset of a process. 1. A thread is a flow of execution within a process.\n2. Each process has its own memory space, while threads share the same memory space. 2. Threads share the same memory space as the process.\n3. Processes are heavyweight, while"
